    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a good start. I think my more conservative students might push back and argue that substantive due process is basically just \"judicial activism\" or that it has no real basis in the Constitution compared to procedural due process. How should I address those criticisms in the lesson? Should I explain why those arguments are considered outdated or incorrect in a modern legal context?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Instead of definitively labeling the arguments \"outdated,\" it is more effective to present them as a strong legal debate for your debate in the class. You can frame the \"judicial activism\" argument by asking the class to define a \"strong preference for the Constitution\" versus \"strong preference for a specific social outcome in the 21st century.\" This shows that the debate is about **how best to interpret the Constitution**, which is the core of constitutional law, rather than a simple right vs. wrong argument.",
      "source": "target",
